(a) Definitions.
Film permit shall mean written authorization from the county manager or his/her designee to conduct filming activity described in the permit.
Filming shall mean the taking of motion pictures, commercials, television, and/or any other filming activity or the use and operation of television, motion picture, or other similar type of cameras on any medium including film, tape or digital format.
News media shall mean the photographing, filming or videotaping for the purpose of spontaneous, unplanned television news broadcast, news gathering, investigative reporting, or reporting for print media by reporters, photographers or cameramen.
Studio shall mean a fixed place of business where filming is regularly conducted upon the premises.
(b) Purpose. This section, including its subsections, has been enacted based upon a recognized need for rules and procedures to govern the issuance of permits for filming activities on public property within unincorporated Cobb County or on any property owned by Cobb County. Film permit fees are required for the administrative costs of the film permit process, to offset the cost of services provided by the county at specific film locations, and to cover the cost for marketing the county as a potential film location, coordinating with the state on specific film initiatives, and providing assistance to film location scouts.
(c) Permits and exemptions.
(1) Permit required. No person or entity shall conduct filming that impacts any public property, in unincorporated Cobb County or property owned by Cobb County without first applying for and receiving a film permit from the county manager or her/his designee.
(2) Exemptions. The following activities do not require that a permit be obtained pursuant to this chapter:
a. News media. The activities of reporters, photographers or camera persons in the employ of a newspaper, news service, or similar entity engaged in on-the-spot print media, publishing or broadcasting, of news events concerning those persons, scenes or occurrences which are in the news and of general public interest;
b. Filming activities conducted for use in a criminal investigation or civil or criminal court proceeding;
c. Private filming activities for personal purposes or for private-family use, including private filming using a professional photographer;
d. Filming activities conducted for public and state, county or municipal access stations and other educational activities.
(d) Rules and regulations. The county manager or her/his designee is hereby authorized and directed to promulgate rules, regulations, and a fee schedule including any applicable insurance requirements governing the form, time, location and conditions of any filming activity that will use, impact, or impede Cobb County lands, right-of-way, or property.
The fee schedule shall include, at a minimum, the following:
(1) Application fee;
(2) County facilities fee (e.g., parks, courthouse, other);
(3) County vehicles fee (e.g., police or fire department vehicles);
(4) County personnel fee;
(5) County right-of-way fee;
(6) Water fee;
(7) Fireworks or explosives fee;
(8) A revision charge for scheduling or location changes to an approved permit, except those caused by the county or by weather; and
(9) A penalty fee for violation of permit conditions.
(e) Permit applications and issuance.
(1) Applications. Applicants for a film permit shall complete and file an application, on a form prescribed by Cobb County, which shall contain detailed information about the applicant and the project. The form must be signed and accompanied by all required fees, deposits, hold harmless agreement, and insurance certificates and policies required by this chapter. Students applying for a film permit shall supply a written certificate from the school of their attendance which shall state that they are bona fide and currently enrolled students of the school. Nonprofit organizations applying shall supply a certificate from the IRS and other taxing authority indicating that they are exempt from taxation under federal and state statutes.
(2) Issuance. Applications for filming must be filed at least ten business days in advance of the time that the proposed film activity is to commence. The deadline for filing an application may be waived for good cause by the county manager or her/his designee if there is sufficient time to process the application and for the county to prepare for the requested filming activity.
The county reserves the right to revoke film permits and filming privileges if:
a. The provisions of this chapter, the permit, or any applicable laws or ordinances are violated; or
b. During the filming activities circumstances arise which are, in the opinion of the county, deleterious to the rights of its residents or businesses or are in any way a danger to the health, safety or welfare of the community.
(f) Location agreement. Upon issuance of a filming permit, if filming on county-owned property, the permittee shall execute a location filming agreement, which is to be furnished by Cobb County. The agreement must be executed by both Cobb County and the applicant prior to filming.
Any filming that occurs upon or impacts county owned property that does not obtain a film permit shall be subject to all state, federal and local regulations and requirements, including all fees and fines, and shall be responsible for all liability arising from such activity.
(Amd. of 2-24-15; Amd. of 7-28-15; Amd. of 10-23-18; Amd. of 11-21-24)

